Test programs
=============

Each test source is a standalone program with its own `main()`. Most use the
shared harness in `test_misc.cpp`:

- `test_handle_args()` parses the file-driver (`-d`) and root (`-r`) options.
- `test_init()` creates the platform hooks, file manager, and logger, and
	mounts the selected file driver.
- `test_deinit()` releases the logger, file manager, and platform hooks.

The tests initialize additional singletons themselves when needed. For
example, `test_file_errors` mounts its fake `TEST` driver after `test_init()`,
and `test_timing_data` creates `PrefsManager` because `TimingData` uses it.

Linux build and run
-------------------

The Linux CMake build defines `test_file_readers`, `test_file_errors`,
`test_timing_data`, and `test_threads` when `BUILD_TESTING` is enabled (it is
enabled by default through CTest). These targets share the engine sources and
compile settings, but omit `Main.cpp` so each test can provide its own `main()`.
CTest runs the tests from the repository root, except `test_file_readers`,
which uses a build-directory fixture folder.

Configure and build the test targets from the repository root:

```sh
cmake -S . -B build -DBUILD_TESTING=ON
cmake --build build --target test_file_readers test_file_errors test_timing_data test_threads --parallel
```

Run them with:

```sh
ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure
```

CTest automatically generates `test_file_readers` fixtures before running
that test. To run its deflate-only path manually, use
`./itgmania-test_file_readers zlib` from the repository root after building;
the CMake output name for this target is `test_file_readers`, so the executable
is `./test_file_readers`.

The old Unix hookup was in `src/Makefile.am`, which was removed with the
Autotools build system. The current CMake targets use the full engine source
set (excluding `Main.cpp`) as a shared object library so test executables get
the same platform implementation and link dependencies as ITGmania.

The old target definitions are useful when restoring the hookup. They shared
these sources with each test: architecture utilities, RageFile, Lua,
architecture hooks, threads, dialogs, `test_misc.cpp`, IniFile, XmlFile,
Command, DateTime, and MessageManager. Per-test additions were:

| Program | Additional sources and libraries |
| --- | --- |
| `test_file_readers` | Shared sources only |
| `test_file_errors` | `MsdFile.cpp`, `CryptManager.cpp`; link `libtomcrypt` and `libtommath` |
| `test_timing_data` | `TimingData.cpp` |
| `test_threads` | Shared sources only |
| `test_audio_readers` | Sound file readers, sound reader/filter implementations, `RageSoundUtil.cpp`, and `RageSoundMixBuffer.cpp`; link audio libraries |

`test_audio_readers` is intentionally not added as a target: its audio fixtures
are not included, so its reader tests may not be usable as-is. Its old note
said the input set was about 30 MB and was not committed.

Runtime notes for when targets are restored
-------------------------------------------

Run the programs from the repository root so relative resource and test-file
paths resolve consistently. `test_file_readers` supports `-c` to generate its
binary and text fixtures; run it again without `-c` to read and check them. Its
`zlib` command-line argument selects the deflate test. `test_file_errors`
generates its input and simulated read/write failures in memory. `test_threads`
exercises Linux thread suspension and backtrace behavior, and therefore
requires the Unix thread/backtrace implementations to be included in its
target.
